# Building SafeLink

This document describes the reference build configuration for SafeLink v0.1.

SafeLink v0.1 is deliberately version-locked. The objective is to reproduce the configuration that has already passed build verification and physical ESP32-P4 / ESP32-C6 hardware testing.

Do not substitute newer versions while attempting the initial reproduction.

# Reference Configuration

## ESP32-P4 Host

```text
Processor:              ESP32-P4
Silicon:                ECO2 / prev3
Arduino-ESP32:          3.3.7
ESP-Hosted host:        2.11.6
esp_wifi_remote:        1.3.2
Transport:              4-bit SDIO
ESP-Hosted mode:        Packet mode
SDIO RX optimisation:   Disabled
```

## ESP32-C6 Communications Processor

```text
Processor:              ESP32-C6
ESP-Hosted slave:       2.12.9
SafeLink C6 changes:    Required
Transport:              4-bit SDIO
```

# Important Version Note

The validated SafeLink v0.1 configuration intentionally uses:

```text
P4 host:   ESP-Hosted 2.11.6
C6 slave:  ESP-Hosted 2.12.9 with SafeLink changes
```

This is the configuration that was physically tested.

It should not be interpreted as a general recommendation to mix arbitrary ESP-Hosted host and slave versions.

The SafeLink C6 modifications and the tested packet-mode configuration form part of this specific compatibility relationship.

# Build Philosophy

SafeLink should be reproduced from a known stock baseline.

The recommended process is:

```text
Known stock source
        |
        v
Verify exact versions
        |
        v
Apply SafeLink patches
        |
        v
Verify configuration
        |
        v
Build
        |
        v
Verify generated libraries
        |
        v
Install into separate Arduino environment
        |
        v
Test on physical hardware
```

Do not modify the only working Arduino installation on a development computer.

A separate build or test installation is strongly recommended.

# 1. Obtain Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7

Install or obtain the official Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 package.

SafeLink v0.1 was built against the ESP32-P4 ECO2 library configuration associated with this release.

The target board configuration must resolve to:

```text
ESP32-P4
Chip variant: prev3
```

for the validated ECO2 target.

Do not use libraries generated for a later ESP32-P4 silicon target in place of the ECO2 / prev3 libraries.

# 2. Confirm the Baseline

Before applying SafeLink, confirm the important component versions.

The expected SafeLink v0.1 P4 baseline is:

```text
Arduino-ESP32                 3.3.7

esp32-arduino-lib-builder     commit 8cabf2c

ESP-IDF release/v5.5          commit 87912cd291

esp_wifi_remote               1.3.2

esp_hosted                    2.11.6
```

These values record the exact source provenance used by the validated SafeLink build.

If the source tree does not correspond to this baseline, stop and determine why before applying the patch.

# 3. Preserve a Stock Control

Before modifying ESP-Hosted, build or retain a stock copy of the ESP32-P4 ECO2 libraries.

The stock build provides a control against which the SafeLink build can be compared.

The reference SafeLink development process first established that the stock build environment reproduced the expected Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 ECO2 library set.

Only after that control passed were the SafeLink modifications introduced.

This is recommended when reproducing SafeLink.

# 4. Apply the P4 SafeLink Patch

The P4 host modifications will be supplied in:

```text
patches/p4/safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

Apply this patch only to the documented ESP-Hosted 2.11.6 host source baseline.

The patch changes the P4 ESP-Hosted transport receive path to implement the SafeLink packet-buffer and back-pressure policy.

A normal Git patch workflow may use:

```bash
git apply --check safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

before making any changes.

If the check succeeds:

```bash
git apply safelink-p4-esp-hosted-2.11.6.patch
```

If `git apply --check` fails, do not force the patch.

A failure usually means the source version is different from the baseline expected by SafeLink.

# 5. Verify SafeLink Configuration

Before compiling, verify the SafeLink invariants.

The resulting configuration must provide:

```text
ESP-Hosted packet mode              ENABLED

SDIO transport                      ENABLED

4-bit SDIO                          CONFIGURED

SDIO RX optimisation                DISABLED

SafeLink fixed packet pool          ENABLED

Bounded packet capacity             ENABLED
```

The exact configuration symbols may depend on the ESP-Hosted source location and build environment.

The behavioural requirements are more important than blindly copying a configuration file.

# 6. Ensure the Patched Component Is Actually Selected

This is an important build check.

ESP-IDF component precedence can result in an unmodified managed component being selected instead of a locally patched copy.

A successful compilation therefore does not, by itself, prove that SafeLink was included.

The build should confirm that the SafeLink-modified ESP-Hosted source files are the files selected by CMake.

The validated SafeLink build explicitly checked source selection before accepting the resulting libraries.

# 7. Build the ESP32-P4 Libraries

Build the ESP32-P4 libraries using the Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 library-builder environment and the ECO2 / prev3 configuration.

The resulting library set should correspond to the normal Arduino ESP32-P4 ECO2 library package, except for differences intentionally introduced by the SafeLink transport policy.

SafeLink should not require unrelated modifications to the Arduino core or the user's application.

# 8. Verify the Result

Do not treat a successful compiler exit as sufficient validation.

At minimum, verify:

```text
Correct target                          ESP32-P4 ECO2 / prev3

Correct Arduino baseline                3.3.7

Correct ESP-Hosted host                 2.11.6

Packet mode                             Correct

SDIO RX optimisation                    Disabled

SafeLink patched source selection       Confirmed

SafeLink implementation in binaries     Confirmed
```

The original SafeLink build process included binary implementation markers specifically so that accidental compilation of the stock ESP-Hosted source could be detected.

# 9. Install the SafeLink P4 Libraries

Install the resulting ESP32-P4 ECO2 library set into a separate Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7 test environment.

Keeping the SafeLink installation separate from the stock installation makes it easy to:

* compare behaviour
* return to the stock configuration
* isolate build problems
* avoid accidentally mixing libraries from different targets or versions

Do not mix SafeLink-generated libraries with libraries from another Arduino-ESP32 release.

# 10. Prepare the ESP32-C6 Firmware

SafeLink v0.1 also requires the corresponding C6-side configuration.

The C6 baseline is:

```text
ESP32-C6
ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
SafeLink packet-mode changes applied
```

The C6 patch will be provided separately as:

```text
patches/c6/safelink-c6-esp-hosted-2.12.9.patch
```

This separation allows the host-side and slave-side modifications to be reviewed independently.

Do not assume that an untouched stock ESP-Hosted 2.12.9 C6 firmware image is equivalent to the SafeLink-tested C6 firmware.

# 11. Apply the C6 Patch

Against the correct ESP-Hosted 2.12.9 slave source:

```bash
git apply --check safelink-c6-esp-hosted-2.12.9.patch
```

If the check succeeds:

```bash
git apply safelink-c6-esp-hosted-2.12.9.patch
```

If the check fails, verify the ESP-Hosted source version rather than forcing the patch.

# 12. Build and Flash the C6

Build the patched ESP32-C6 ESP-Hosted slave firmware using the configuration supplied with the SafeLink v0.1 patch set.

Flash the resulting slave firmware to the ESP32-C6 using the normal ESP-Hosted / ESP-IDF flashing procedure appropriate to the target hardware.

Preserve a copy of the original working C6 firmware before replacing it.

# 13. First Hardware Test

After both sides are prepared:

```text
SafeLink ESP32-P4 host
          |
          | 4-bit SDIO
          |
SafeLink ESP32-C6 slave
```

perform a basic communication test before integrating SafeLink into a large application.

Confirm:

* C6 startup
* SDIO initialization
* P4/C6 communication
* Wi-Fi initialization
* network scan
* connection to an access point
* network traffic
* continued SDIO operation

# 14. Application Integration

SafeLink is below the application networking layer.

An application should not need to contain SafeLink-specific networking logic merely to benefit from the modified ESP-Hosted transport.

The application used for the original SafeLink validation is private and is not required to reproduce SafeLink.

SafeLink should therefore be tested first at the transport/network level and then inside the developer's own application.

# Reference Build Principle

The initial reproduction should always follow:

```text
Arduino-ESP32 3.3.7
        +
P4 ESP-Hosted 2.11.6
        +
SafeLink P4 patch
        +
ESP32-P4 ECO2 / prev3
        |
        | 4-bit SDIO
        |
ESP32-C6
        +
ESP-Hosted slave 2.12.9
        +
SafeLink C6 patch
```

Once this known configuration has been reproduced, developers may investigate ports to newer releases.

A newer-version port should be considered a separate implementation until its SafeLink invariants and runtime behaviour have been independently verified on physical hardware.
